WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.530
Film Courage: &nbsp;When do you start writing
everyday?

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:07.509
Danny Strong, Screenwriter/Actor: So I have
basically the same pattern when I’m writing.

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:10.839
Sometimes if I’m in production it will change.

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:15.940
&nbsp;But if I’m just in a writing phase (which
is my favorite to be honest with you because

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:22.869
I love my schedule as a writer) I wake up,
I go somewhere, have breakfast and usually

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:28.760
I can go somewhere and write and then I’ll
open my computer…so now it’s around 10:30ish

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.860
and then I return emails for about an hour
or an hour and a half, and then I start writing.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.330
And then I write for about three to four hours
straight and then I’m done.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.610
And that is basically my process.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.140
And sometimes it takes me about two hours
to get started and then when I get to that

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.870
two hour mark and haven’t started that’s
when self-loathing comes in, so then I do

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:49.870
get started.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.061
But usually I can get going in about an hour.

00:00:51.061 --> 00:00:52.510
But usually I can get going to about an hour
to an hour and a half.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:56.100
Film Courage: Is this on a computer that is
hooked up to the Internet?

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.510
Danny Strong: Yeah, I definitely stay on the
Internet because I’m looking things up all

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.300
the time as I’m writing.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.050
Sometimes it’s just random things…like
I’m looking for a synonym or something.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:15.290
And I’ll just got to synonym and type in
a word and some synonyms will pop up.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.430
So it can be as simple as that.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.610
Film Courage: So it’s not a distraction?

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:21.610
Danny Strong: No, not on the whole.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.540
I mean it can be a little bit but it works
for me.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.530
And so that’s my schedule.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.220
Film Courage: What’s the longest writing
day you’ve ever had?

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:36.370
Danny Strong: I don’t like to write once
I get tired because then I just lose my focus

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.890
and the writing is not as good.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:45.210
But the first two seasons on Empire I wrote
a lot of the show.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:51.070
So those would turn into sometimes long days
where you’re writing and rewriting an episode

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:54.400
and you’re doing a lot of work so sometimes
you’re doing a lot of work so sometimes

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.400
10 hours.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.100
And I don’t like it at all and I think the
work suffers for it.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.270
Film Courage: So you think you’re better
at the 3-4 hour mark?

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.270
Danny Strong: Yeah!

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:02.270
That’s why I do it.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.729
It’s literally as soon as I start to lose
my focus, then I just stop.

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:09.429
And I don’t have writer’s block.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.239
It’s not a very dramatic thing.

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:13.989
There’s no inspiration, I don’t wait for
inspiration.

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:16.439
I say inspiration is for amateurs.

00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:19.400
Amateurs get inspired, professionals just
go do it.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.719
And it’s not even very dramatic anymore.

00:02:21.719 --> 00:02:25.080
I mean I just go and I know I’m going to
do it.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.060
I mean I know I’m going to get stuff done.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.969
And it’s very productive to be honest with
you.

00:02:29.969 --> 00:02:34.909
I find that I get a lot of writing done because
I don’t make a big deal about it.

00:02:34.909 --> 00:02:37.120
It’s not this big dramatic thing for me.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.129
I just show up and I just get going.

00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:43.219
Like I said, it takes sometimes an hour and
hour and a half to actually get started.

00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:45.560
Occasionally two hours!

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.870
And then we just start going and I try to
be non-judgmental of it.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:54.670
And I don’t sit there and question it or
think that I’m not talented or…I just

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.480
try and do it.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:04.279
And then there’s a phase when you have to
edit it and go through it and that is a more

00:03:04.279 --> 00:03:06.500
critical part of your brain.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.820
But I do my best not to beat myself up about
it.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.750
And if I don’t like something I just change
it and I just keep working on it and working

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.229
on it and not turn it into some big melodramatic,
artist’s struggle.

00:03:18.229 --> 00:03:20.059
I don’t view it that way at all.

00:03:20.059 --> 00:03:25.959
I will say one thing that I think could be
very interesting to people that are writers

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:35.169
is what I do is I outline a script very meticulously
and my outlines are about 25 pages to 30 pages

00:03:35.169 --> 00:03:36.169
long.

00:03:36.169 --> 00:03:42.199
And then when I go to write the actual first
draft, I will not reread one word of what

00:03:42.199 --> 00:03:45.790
I’ve written until I’ve written the whole
script.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:51.010
So for my first pass on a screenplay, I start
from the beginning and then I just go…and

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:56.549
I go all the way through until I’m done
without having read one word of it and it

00:03:56.549 --> 00:03:58.419
takes about three weeks.

00:03:58.419 --> 00:04:00.963
So in three weeks, I’ve written a whole
draft and it’s a whole draft and it’s

00:04:00.963 --> 00:04:03.919
a lot of fun because there is no judgment
whatsoever.

00:04:03.919 --> 00:04:06.309
I’m just writing straight through.

00:04:06.309 --> 00:04:12.819
And then I go through and very meticulously
work through the scenes and that takes two

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:17.989
to three weeks and then in six weeks I have
a draft but it’s really two drafts because

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:22.930
I’ve done one straight through and then
one (this sort of meticulous work-through).

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:27.729
And I will say that the second phase of it
(the meticulous work-through phase) is the

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:32.940
least enjoyable part of the process because
it’s the most critical where for a couple

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:38.510
weeks I just I have to work through these
scenes but I just do it.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.370
And then after that I have a draft to work
off of and then there are many drafts after

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:43.370
that.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.009
But I have this nice foundation.

00:04:45.009 --> 00:04:49.289
Film Courage: Going back to what you said
about not making a big deal about waiting

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:52.289
for inspiration, was it always this way for
you?

00:04:52.289 --> 00:04:56.030
Danny Strong: No this was over many years
of figuring this out.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:00.430
And for the first several years I was writing,
I was deep in my acting career and writing

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:07.419
was more therapeutic to get my mind off of
the very painful trials and tribulations of

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:11.780
an acting career because it’s very difficult
and you get rejected a lot.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:19.710
And all of the cliches are absolutely true
but I was serious about it but I think it

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:25.312
just took awhile of realizing that beating
yourself up and I have no talent and this

00:05:25.312 --> 00:05:31.580
is terrible, I’ll work with a writer and
they’ll say “Oh that was sh*t!”

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.190
And I’ll say “Don’t worry about it.

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:38.810
Don’t worry about if it’s good or bad,
just take a look at it and see what could

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:39.810
be improved.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:44.139
There’s no reason to be so caustic and hostile
about the material.

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:46.539
It’s just words on a screen.

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:48.680
You can change them.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:49.960
Just change them.”

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:54.000
So that’s very much something that guides
me in my work.

